"The coming of Allah's command has two meanings: The first is the singular of 'amr' meaning the divine acts of Allah, in which case the punishment promised to the disbelievers or the Day of Resurrection is meant. The second is the singular of 'amr' meaning Allah's decree and commandment. Both meanings have been interpreted in exegesis. The expression 'do not seek to hasten it' indicates that this command has not yet arrived, but is about to come. For if it had already arrived, seeking to hasten it would be meaningless.\n\nIn the second verse, it is commanded that Allah sends down the angels with a spirit (revelation) of His command upon whom He wills among His servants, and that they convey to mankind the message: 'There is no deity except Me, so fear Me alone.' This is a definition of revelation: Revelation is a spiritual matter from Allah's commands. All revelations are summarized in two fundamental principles: Tawhid (intellectual perfection) and taqwa (practical perfection).\n\nAllah created the heavens and the earth in truth; everything has a reality. He created man from a sperm-drop, yet man became an open adversary against his Lord. He also created livestock; in them there is warmth, benefits, and food. Riding animals such as horses, mules, and donkeys were created both for riding and for adornment. Allah has taken upon Himself to guide to the right path; had He willed, He would have guided everyone.\n\nAllah sends down water from the sky, and with it He produces drinking water and plants. The night, the day, the sun, the moon, and the stars have been subjected to the service of man. Fresh meat and ornaments are extracted from the sea. Mountains have been set firm upon the earth, and rivers and roads have been created. All of these are lessons for people who reflect and take heed.\n\nThe Creator and the non-creator cannot be equal. The blessings of Allah cannot be counted. The idols that the polytheists worship cannot create anything; they are dead. Your God is One God. The hearts of those who do not believe in the Hereafter are denialist. The disbelievers will be disgraced on the Day of Resurrection, and it will be asked: 'Where are My partners?' Those who possess taqwa (the God-conscious), on the other hand, will enter the Gardens of Eden beneath which rivers flow, and the angels will greet them.\n\nTo every nation a messenger was sent, saying, 'Worship Allah and shun false gods.' Allah guides whom He wills and leaves astray whom He wills. The disbelievers swear that Allah will not resurrect the dead. Yet, this is a true promise of Allah. When He intends a thing, He only says to it, 'Be,' and it is.\n\nTo those who emigrated after suffering oppression, a good abode will be granted in this world. Men were also sent as messengers before you. The Quran was sent down so that you may clarify to the people what has been revealed to them. Let not those who devise insidious plots feel secure from Allah's punishment. Even the shadows of everything Allah created prostrate, submitting to Him. The angels, too, prostrate to Allah and do as they are commanded.\n\nAllah commands: 'Do not take two gods; fear Me alone.' Whatever is in the heavens and on earth belongs to Him. Every blessing is from Allah. The polytheists allocate a portion of their provision to idols; they attribute daughters to Allah, while they themselves bury female children. For those who do not believe in the Hereafter are evil attributes, whereas for Allah are the most exalted attributes. Allah does not immediately punish people for their wrongdoing, but defers them until an appointed term.\n\nThe honeybee was inspired to take for itself dwellings in the mountains, trees, and hives, then to eat from all the fruits and follow the ways made smooth by its Lord. From the belly of the bee comes forth a drink of varying colors in which there is healing for mankind. In these verses there are great lessons for a people who reflect. From date palms and grapevines you obtain both an intoxicant and wholesome nourishment; this indicates that intoxicants had not yet been forbidden.\n\nAllah Almighty states in verse 69 of Surah an-Nahl that He inspired the honeybee. The commentators have interpreted this revelation as inspiration (ilham), since it is not the revelation of prophethood. However, the fact that the art of making honey was bestowed upon the bee with unerring necessity evokes the power and precision of the revelation that came to the prophets. That is to say, this innate disposition (fitrah) given to the bee was instilled into its spirit through nurture and instruction.\n\nIn verses 70–74, Allah's power to create, cause death, and cause some to live to the most decrepit age of life is described. Although He has favored some over others in provision, those who are favored do not hand over their provision to those under their possession so that equality is achieved. Allah granted you spouses from your own kind, along with sons and grandsons, and sustained you with lawful provisions. In spite of this, the polytheists believe in falsehood and deny the blessing of Allah, worshiping idols that possess no provision from the heavens or the earth. Do not set up partners with Allah, for He knows and you do not know.\n\nIn verses 75–76, two parables are presented. The first: an enslaved servant incapable of doing anything, and a free person whom We have provided with good provision, who spends from it secretly and openly. They cannot be equal. Thus, those who worship other than Allah are like slaves, whereas those who believe in the oneness of Allah are free. The second parable: a mute man incapable of doing anything, who is a burden to his master, and a man who commands justice and is on a straight path. These two are not equal either. The disbeliever resembles the former, and the believer resembles the latter.\n\nIn verses 77–79, the knowledge of the unseen (ghayb) in the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah. The Day of Resurrection will occur as swiftly as the blink of an eye; Allah is capable of all things. He brought you out from the bellies of your mothers while you knew nothing, and granted you hearing, sight, and hearts so that you might give thanks. Have they not looked at the birds held suspended in the air of the sky, obeying Allah's command? In this there are signs for a people who believe. This verse seems to support that experience and habituation are effective in acquiring knowledge.\n\nIn verses 80–83, Allah's blessings are enumerated: He made your homes places of rest, and provided you with light houses (tents) from the hides of livestock, and from their wool and hair furnishings and trade goods for a time. He created shelters in the mountains, garments to protect you from heat, and coats of armor for battle. He completes His favor upon you so that you may submit to Him. If they turn away, your duty is only conveyance. They recognize the blessing of Allah, then they deny it; most of them are disbelievers.\n\nIn verses 84–89, it is explained that on the Day of Resurrection a witness will be brought from every community, the disbelievers will not be permitted to speak, nor will their punishment be alleviated. When the polytheists see their idols, they will say, 'Our Lord, these are our partners,' but the idols will reply, 'Indeed, you are liars.' On that day, they will surrender to Allah, and all that they fabricated will vanish from them. For those who disbelieved and prevented others from the way of Allah, We will increase punishment upon punishment. We shall bring from every community a witness from among themselves, and We will bring you as a witness over them. We have sent down the Book as an explanation of all things, a guide, a mercy, and glad tidings.\n\nVerse 90 is of utmost importance: Allah commands justice, benevolence, and giving to relatives; He forbids indecency, wickedness, and oppression. Justice means placing everything in its proper place and believing in the oneness of Allah. Ihsan means doing work excellently and doing good to people. Fahsha refers to sins of excessive lust such as adultery/fornication; munkar refers to evils unrecognized by the Shariah and sound custom; and baghy is oppression and injustice. This verse encompasses good and evil in the most comprehensive manner. Among the Companions, Uthman ibn Maz'un and Ibn Mas'ud emphasized the significance of this verse.\n\nIn verses 91–97, fulfilling the covenant of Allah and not breaking oaths are commanded. Do not make oaths a means of deceit for the sake of one community being more numerous than another, and do not be like the woman who untwists her yarn after spinning it strongly. Had Allah willed, He would have made you all a single community; He leads astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. Do not use oaths for deceit lest your foot slip. Do not exchange the covenant of Allah for a small price; the reward with Allah is far better. Whatever is with you will be exhausted, while what is with Allah is everlasting. Whoever does righteous deeds—whether male or female—while being a believer will certainly be granted a good life and the finest reward.\n\nVerses 98–100 command that when one wishes to recite the Quran, one should first seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan. Satan has no authority over those who believe and place their trust in their Lord. Satan's authority applies only to those who take him as an ally and associate partners with Allah. Uttering the formula 'a'udhu' (seeking refuge) orally is not obligatory (wajib), but recommended (mustahabb).\n\nIn verses 101–105, the subject of abrogation (naskh) is addressed: When We replace one verse with another, the disbelievers say, 'You are only a fabricator.' Allah knows best what He reveals. Abrogation occurs with knowledge and wisdom; both the prior and subsequent verses serve the welfare of the servants. Gabriel (the Holy Spirit / Ruh al-Qudus) brought down the Quran in truth, to grant steadfastness to those who believe and as guidance and good tidings for the Muslims. The disbelievers said, 'A human is teaching him.' Yet the tongue of the person they allege is foreign, while the Quran is in clear Arabic. Allah does not guide those who do not believe in the verses of Allah, and for them is a painful punishment. Only those who do not believe in the verses invent falsehood.\n\nThe deniers slandered the Prophet by saying, 'A human is teaching the Quran.' This claim is rationally inconsistent; for if the person assumed to be teaching was not an Arab, how could he teach such exquisite Arabic? And had he been an Arab, he would have had to come forward and issue a challenge. Yet no such person is known. This claim amounts to accepting a twofold extraordinary feat and is contradictory.\n\nThose who do not believe in the verses of Allah cannot find the right way, and for them there is a painful punishment. Only these deniers invent lies; the Prophet is not a liar. The Quran is the true Book brought down by Gabriel from the Lord's presence. Believers must not be deceived by the words of the deniers and fall into disbelief.\n\nThose who revert to disbelief after having believed incur the wrath of Allah, except for those who are coerced while their hearts remain full of faith and utter disbelief only with their tongues. The family of 'Ammar ibn Yasir was killed under torture, and when he was coerced, he uttered words of disbelief with his tongue, and the Prophet granted him permission. This is a dispensation (rukhsah); however, as a matter of resolute steadfastness ('azimah), risking death and resisting is more virtuous. Those who open their hearts to disbelief are the ones who prefer the life of this world over the Hereafter.\n\nAllah is the helper of those who emigrated after being wronged, who strove, and who were patient. Allah sets forth the parable of a city that was secure and abundantly provided for; when its people were ungrateful, hunger and fear befell them. Eat from the lawful and wholesome provisions, and be grateful for the blessing of Allah. What has been forbidden are: carrion, blood, swine flesh, and animals slaughtered in the name of other than Allah. Whoever is compelled by necessity, without desiring it or exceeding limits, may eat.\n\nDo not say with your own tongues, 'This is lawful, and that is unlawful,' for this is fabricating lies against Allah. There is brief enjoyment in this world, and in the Hereafter a painful punishment. Certain things had previously been forbidden to the Jews; they wronged themselves. Allah forgives those who commit a sin out of ignorance, then repent and mend their ways.\n\nAbraham (Ibrahim) was an upright leader (hanif), obedient to Allah, and he was not of the polytheists. Allah chose him and guided him to a straight path. We granted him good in this world, and in the Hereafter he is among the righteous. It was revealed to the Prophet to follow the upright religion of Abraham. The Sabbath observance was made obligatory only for the Jews who differed concerning it; it does not exist in the religion of Abraham. On the Day of Resurrection, Allah will judge regarding their disputes.\n\nCall to the way of your Lord with wisdom and goodly exhortation, and debate with them in the best manner. Allah knows best who strays from His path and who is guided. If you punish, then punish with an equivalent of that with which you were afflicted; but if you are patient, it is certainly better. Patience is only with the help of Allah. Let the Prophet not grieve over them, nor be distressed by their plotting. Allah is with those who have taqwa (God-consciousness) and those who do good."